{"qid":"Q1991742","names":["Kingdom of Alba"],"title":"Kingdom of Alba","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Kingdom of Alba was the Gaelic-speaking monarchy of northern Britain that coalesced in the late 9th century, traditionally dated to the reign of Constantine II (900–943), when it controlled territory north of the Firth of Forth. Under Máel Coluim I and his successors the kingdom expanded southward, annexing Strathclyde by around 1018 and pushing its border toward the Tweed and Solway. By the late 11th century, under Malcolm III (Máel Coluim mac Donnchada), the realm was recognisably evolving into what contemporaries would call Scotland.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The territory of the Kingdom of Alba is reconstructed from sparse chronicle references, Gaelic poetry, and ecclesiastical records; southern boundaries in particular are debated among historians.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Kingdom of Alba","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kingdom_of_Alba"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Constantine II of Scotland","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Constantine_II_of_Scotland"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Malcolm III of Scotland","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malcolm_III_of_Scotland"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Kingdom of Alba","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Constantine II","nativeName":"Causantín mac Áeda","roman":"","start":900,"end":943,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Malcolm I","nativeName":"Máel Coluim mac Domnaill","roman":"","start":943,"end":954,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Indulf","nati